      :root {
        --font-family-primary: 'Suisse Intl', -apple-system, BlinkMacSystemFont, 'Segoe UI', system-ui, sans-serif;
        /* Mirrors DEFAULT_TOKENS.typography.fontFamilyDisplay so CSS files can
           reach the display face too. Only ever paired with weight 500 — see
           the @font-face note above. Read by canvas-tools/canvas-type.css. */
        --font-family-display: 'KMR Apparat', 'Suisse Intl', -apple-system, BlinkMacSystemFont, 'Segoe UI', system-ui, sans-serif;
        --font-family-mono: 'SF Mono', 'Menlo', 'Monaco', 'Courier New', monospace;
      }
      * {
        margin: 0;
        padding: 0;
        box-sizing: border-box;
      }
      html, body, #root {
        width: 100%;
        height: 100%;
        overflow: hidden;
        /* Stop two-finger horizontal trackpad swipes from triggering browser
           back/forward navigation (which throws users out of the canvas). The
           <canvas> already preventDefaults its own wheels, but swipes over the
           surrounding HTML chrome (margins, panels, cards) leak to the browser;
           this document-root rule blocks the navigation gesture everywhere. */
        overscroll-behavior: none;
        background: #000000;
        font-family: var(--font-family-primary);
        font-size: 12px;
        font-weight: 400;
        -webkit-font-smoothing: antialiased;
        -moz-osx-font-smoothing: grayscale;
      }
      /* The use-case doors are light from their first frame (the head script above sets
         `data-boot-ground`): #F8F8F8 is the door's ground, `P.surface` in login-frame.ts. */
      html[data-boot-ground='light'],
      html[data-boot-ground='light'] body,
      html[data-boot-ground='light'] #root {
        background: #F8F8F8;
      }

      /* Tablet: a long press is OUR context-menu gesture (see
         src/lib/touch-context-menu.ts — it is the only door to Delete,
         Duplicate, Rerun and the rest on a device with no right-click). iOS
         answers the same press with its native callout ("Save Image", "Copy"),
         which would cover our menu, so suppress it.

         Scoped to `(hover: none) and (pointer: coarse)` on purpose: that is
         false on a touchscreen LAPTOP, where the primary input is still a mouse
         and the native callout should be left alone. Text fields keep their
         callout regardless — the JS bails out on inputs and contenteditable, so
         cursor placement, Select All and Paste behave normally in a sticky note. */
      @media (hover: none) and (pointer: coarse) {
        #root {
          -webkit-touch-callout: none;
        }

        /* Overlay cards (sticky notes, image/video refs, shapes, frames, text,
           chains, palettes...) are dragged by finger through DragManager's
           pointer events. On a touchscreen a drag that starts on a
           `touch-action: auto` element is claimed by the BROWSER as a possible
           scroll: it fires `pointercancel` a few moves in and the card never
           moves. Measured on the real input pipeline, where the whole overlay
           ancestry computed to `touch-action: auto`.

           Tool cards are excluded and handle it on their header instead
           (ToolCardHeader `S.header`). `touch-action` cannot be re-enabled by a
           descendant, so setting `none` on a tool card's ROOT would also kill
           scrolling inside its prompt textarea and settings panel. Every other
           overlay type drags from its whole body and has nothing to scroll. */
        [data-overlay-id]:not([data-overlay-type='tool-card']) {
          touch-action: none;
        }
      }

      /* Text selection: neutral mid-gray tint at 50% so the highlight is
         visible on BOTH light and dark surfaces while staying quiet. The text
         color is left untouched so selected text stays readable.
         Was currentColor-based ("adapt to the text's own color"), but Chromium
         paints ::selection currentColor as a dark tint regardless of the
         element's text color, which made selection completely invisible in
         every light-text-on-dark input (settings, admin dark, home panels).
         Do NOT reintroduce currentColor here. */
      ::selection {
        background-color: rgba(128, 128, 128, 0.5);
      }
      ::-moz-selection {
        background-color: rgba(128, 128, 128, 0.5);
      }


      /* =====================================================================
         BOOT SKELETON — the dashboard's first frame, before any JS runs.
         =====================================================================

         ⚠ THIS IS A HAND COPY OF `DashboardSkeleton.tsx` AND HAS TO STAY ONE.
         Every number below is that component's, which is `dashboard-frame.ts`'s:
         the 219 menu, the 260 rail, the 54 identity row, the 200-minimum card
         track. If either side moves, the handoff at React mount becomes a
         visible jump, which is the whole thing this was built to stop. There is
         a matching warning at the top of that file.

         ⚠ AND IT CANNOT BE THE SAME CODE, which is the honest reason for the
         duplication. React is what renders that component, and the gap being
         covered here is the one BEFORE React exists — the entry bundle is still
         downloading, `#root` is empty, and the page is the black in `html, body`
         below. Operator, 2026-08-28: *"i always see a black screen then the
         skeleton then the app - i want to see the skeleton right away"*.

         React clears `#root` on its first commit, so nothing removes this: the
         real skeleton takes its place in the same frame, drawn identically.

         The unit is `--tc-u` verbatim from `chrome-metrics.ts`, so this scales
         with the viewport exactly as the app does. Radii carry the x2
         SMOOTHING_BOOST the app's `squircle()` applies. */
      #boot-skeleton { display: none; }
      html[data-boot-skeleton='on'] #boot-skeleton { display: flex; }
      #boot-skeleton {
        --tc-u: clamp(1px, 0.06944vw, 1.3333px);
        --bs-u: var(--tc-u);
        /* ⚠ LOW ON PURPOSE, AND A PREVIOUS SESSION ALREADY RAISED THEM AND PUT
           THEM BACK. They were tried at 0.14 / 0.22 and rejected: operator,
           2026-08-28, *"its too different, like there is too much contrast"*.
           The sweep below lights them a little as it passes; the resting ink
           stays this quiet. */
        --bs-block: rgba(248, 245, 238, 0.055);
        --bs-strong: rgba(248, 245, 238, 0.085);
        --bs-hairline: rgba(248, 245, 238, 0.1);
        position: absolute;
        inset: 0;
        flex-direction: column;
        background: #050505;
        overflow: hidden;
      }
      #boot-skeleton i {
        display: block;
        background-color: var(--bs-block);
        border-radius: calc(8 * var(--bs-u));
        corner-shape: squircle;
        flex-shrink: 0;
      }
      #boot-skeleton i.s { background-color: var(--bs-strong); }

      /* ⚠ THIS BLOCK DRIVES BOTH SKELETONS, ON PURPOSE.
         `DashboardSkeleton.tsx` and `skeleton-kit.tsx` put `bs-blk` / `bs-thumb`
         on their own divs so they are styled by the rules below rather than by
         a second copy injected from React. Renaming anything here breaks the
         React skeleton SILENTLY: nothing throws, the blocks just stop moving.

         ONE LIGHT, TRAVELLING LEFT TO RIGHT ACROSS THE WHOLE PAGE. Dor,
         2026-10-03: *"animation should happen from left to right like the
         norm"*. This replaces the 2026-08-28 pulse, where every block breathed
         in place on a hand-shuffled third of three offsets so the page never
         read as a wave. A sweep IS the wave, so the offset tables went with it.

         `background-attachment: fixed` is what makes it one light: every block
         paints its own slice of a single viewport-wide band, so the band
         crosses the menu, the cards and the rail as one sheen, in step, with
         no per-block offset to keep. The stops are a raised cosine (17 of
         them): a straight-line gradient shows a faint edge wherever its slope
         changes, and this one has none, so it reads as light, not a stripe.

         The blocks keep their ink as `background-color`. ⚠ A `background:`
         shorthand on a block (in this file or inline from React) resets the
         band and that block goes still.

         The curve is cube-motion's one curve (`lib/morph-motion.ts §
         MORPH_EASE`) with its last crawl cut off: the light comes in quickly
         from the left and is still moving as it leaves on the right, and the
         next pass starts at once. cube has no looping move, so its curve is
         what it lends here.

         ⚠ THE CURVE IS TRIMMED ON PURPOSE, AND THE FULL ONE PARKS THE LIGHT.
         cube's (0.2, 0, 0, 1) spends its last 40% of the time on its last 3%
         of the distance. Played whole on a loop, with a rest after it, the
         bright part crossed in 450ms and the page then sat dark for 1350ms.
         Dor, 2026-10-03: *"shimmering needs to happen more often - its kinda
         hold every shimmer"*. So the curve is split where cube's own curve
         reaches t = 0.9 (de Casteljau) and the first piece is scaled back to
         the unit square: cubic-bezier(0.245, 0, 0.049, 0.833) IS cube's curve
         over that stretch, not an approximation. Over 1200ms the light crosses
         in about 500ms and the dark gap is about 700ms. Restoring 0.2, 0, 0, 1
         or a rest brings the hold back.

         The React skeletons pick the band up where this one left it:
         `skeleton-kit.tsx § useSweepClock` puts every sweep on the boot
         skeleton's clock, so the hand-over between the two does not move the
         light. */
      @keyframes bs-sweep {
        from { background-position: -50vw 0; }
        to   { background-position: 100vw 0; }
      }
      #boot-skeleton i,
      .bs-thumb,
      .bs-blk {
        background-image: linear-gradient(90deg,
          rgba(248, 245, 238, 0) 0%,
          rgba(248, 245, 238, 0.0019) 6.25%,
          rgba(248, 245, 238, 0.0073) 12.5%,
          rgba(248, 245, 238, 0.0154) 18.75%,
          rgba(248, 245, 238, 0.025) 25%,
          rgba(248, 245, 238, 0.0346) 31.25%,
          rgba(248, 245, 238, 0.0427) 37.5%,
          rgba(248, 245, 238, 0.0481) 43.75%,
          rgba(248, 245, 238, 0.05) 50%,
          rgba(248, 245, 238, 0.0481) 56.25%,
          rgba(248, 245, 238, 0.0427) 62.5%,
          rgba(248, 245, 238, 0.0346) 68.75%,
          rgba(248, 245, 238, 0.025) 75%,
          rgba(248, 245, 238, 0.0154) 81.25%,
          rgba(248, 245, 238, 0.0073) 87.5%,
          rgba(248, 245, 238, 0.0019) 93.75%,
          rgba(248, 245, 238, 0) 100%);
        background-size: 50vw 100vh;
        background-repeat: no-repeat;
        background-attachment: fixed;
        background-position: -50vw 0;
        animation: bs-sweep 1200ms cubic-bezier(0.245, 0, 0.049, 0.833) infinite;
      }

      @media (prefers-reduced-motion: reduce) {
        #boot-skeleton i, .bs-thumb, .bs-blk { animation: none; background-image: none; }
      }
      .bs-ticker { height: calc(18 * var(--bs-u)); flex-shrink: 0; background: #262624; }
      .bs-row { display: flex; flex: 1; min-height: 0; width: 100%; overflow: hidden; }

